    "0the outer atoms. 5. Put leftovers on\n"
    "0the central atom. 6. If the center is\n"
    "0short, make double or triple bonds\n"
    "0using outer lone pairs.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1The octet rule\n"
    "0Most atoms want 8 valence electrons.\n"
    "0Hydrogen wants 2.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Exceptions\n"
    "3H and He: 2 electrons\n"
    "3Be: often 4, B and Al: often 6\n"
    "3Period 3 and beyond can expand past\n"
    "3\0018 (PCl5, SF6) using d orbitals\n"
    "3Odd electron counts (NO, NO2)\n"
    "3\001cannot satisfy every octet\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Formal charge\n"
    "2FC = valence - lone - (bonds)\n"
    "0The best structure keeps formal\n"
    "0charges near zero, and any negative\n"
    "0charge on the most electronegative\n"
    "0atom.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Resonance\n"
    "0When several equivalent structures\n"
    "0are possible (ozone, nitrate,\n"
    "0benzene), the real molecule is an\n"
    "0average of them, not a flicker\n"
    "0between them.";

static const char body_3_3[] =
    "0Electron groups repel and spread as\n"
    "0far apart as possible. Count bonding\n"
    "0groups (a double bond counts once)\n"
    "0plus lone pairs on the central atom.\n"
    "0\n"
    "12 groups: linear, 180\n"
    "20 lone: linear (CO2)\n"
    "0\n"
    "13 groups: trigonal planar, 120\n"
    "20 lone: trigonal planar (BF3)\n"
    "21 lone: bent, about 118 (SO2)\n"
    "0\n"
    "14 groups: tetrahedral, 109.5\n"
    "20 lone: tetrahedral (CH4)\n"
    "21 lone: trigonal pyramidal (NH3, 107)\n"
    "22 lone: bent (H2O, 104.5)\n"
    "0\n"
    "15 groups: trig. bipyramidal, 90/120\n"
    "20 lone: trigonal bipyramidal (PCl5)\n"
    "21 lone: seesaw\n"
    "22 lone: T-shaped\n"
    "23 lone: linear\n"
    "0\n"
    "16 groups: octahedral, 90\n"
    "20 lone: octahedral (SF6)\n"
    "21 lone: square pyramidal\n"
    "22 lone: square planar\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Lone pairs squeeze\n"
    "0Lone pairs take more room than\n"
    "0bonding pairs, so each one shrinks\n"
    "0the bond angles by roughly 2 degrees.";

static const char body_3_4[] =
    "0A molecule is polar if it has polar\n"
    "0bonds AND they do not cancel.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Two-step test\n"
    "01. Are the bonds polar? Check the\n"
    "0electronegativity differences. 2. Do\n"
    "0the dipoles cancel by symmetry?\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Symmetric, so nonpolar\n"
    "2CO2 (linear)\n"
    "2CH4, CCl4 (tetrahedral)\n"
    "2BF3 (trigonal planar)\n"
    "2SF6 (octahedral)\n"
    "0Identical outer atoms arranged\n"
    "0symmetrically always cancel.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Asymmetric, so polar\n"
    "2H2O (bent)\n"
    "2NH3 (trigonal pyramidal)\n"
    "2CHCl3 (different outer atoms)\n"
    "0A lone pair on the central atom\n"
    "0almost always makes a molecule polar.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Dipole moment\n"
    "0Points from the positive toward the\n"
    "0negative end. Measured in debyes.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Why it matters\n"
    "0Like dissolves like. Polar solvents\n"
    "0dissolve polar and ionic solutes;\n"
    "0nonpolar solvents dissolve nonpolar\n"
    "0solutes.";

static const char body_3_5[] =
    "0Forces BETWEEN molecules, far weaker\n"
    "0than the bonds inside them. They set\n"
    "0melting and boiling points.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Weakest to strongest\n"
    "01. London dispersion forces 2.\n"
    "0Dipole-dipole 3. Hydrogen bonding 4.\n"
    "0Ion-dipole\n"
    "0\n"
    "1London dispersion\n"
    "0Present in everything. Caused by\n"
    "0momentary uneven electron\n"
    "0distribution. Stronger for larger,\n"
    "0heavier, more polarizable molecules,\n"
    "0which is why boiling point rises down\n"
    "0the halogens.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Dipole-dipole\n"
    "0Between permanent dipoles in polar\n"
    "0molecules.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Hydrogen bonding\n"
    "0A special strong dipole-dipole when H\n"
    "0is bonded to N, O or F and attracted\n"
    "0to a lone pair on another N, O or F.\n"
    "0Responsible for water's high boiling\n"
    "0point, ice floating, and the shape of\n"
    "0DNA and proteins.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Ion-dipole\n"
    "0Between an ion and a polar molecule.\n"
    "0This is what dissolves salt in water.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Consequences\n"
    "0Stronger intermolecular forces mean\n"
    "0higher boiling point, higher\n"
    "0viscosity, higher surface tension and\n"
    "0lower vapor pressure.";

static const char body_3_6[] =
    "0A lattice of cations in a \"sea\" of\n"
    "0delocalized valence electrons that\n"
    "0belong to the whole crystal.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Explains the properties\n"
    "3Conducts electricity: the electrons\n"
    "3\001move freely, as a solid AND as a\n"
    "3\001liquid\n"
    "3Conducts heat: mobile electrons\n"
    "3\001carry energy\n"
    "3Malleable and ductile: layers slide\n"
    "3\001without breaking bonds, unlike\n"
    "3\001brittle ionic solids\n"
    "3Shiny: free electrons re-emit light\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Strength\n"
    "0More valence electrons and smaller\n"
    "0cations give stronger bonding and\n"
    "0higher melting points. Sodium melts\n"
    "0at 98 C, tungsten at 3422 C.\n"
    "0\n"
    "1Alloys\n"
    "0Solid solutions of metals. Different\n"
    "0sized atoms disrupt the sliding\n"
    "0layers, so alloys are usually harder\n"
    "0than the pure metals. Steel is iron\n"
    "0plus carbon; brass is copper plus\n"
    "0zinc.";

